1
00:00:00,855 --> 00:00:04,203
This is Shurokkha, which means protection in Bangla.

2
00:00:04,203 --> 00:00:12,055
It is an AI trust and financial-safety copilot for upay, built for the DIU CPC and upay AI Hackathon.

3
00:00:12,055 --> 00:00:16,000
Its goal is simple: stop the scam before the money leaves.

4
00:00:16,926 --> 00:00:28,530
For first-time mobile wallet users, prize scams, fake wrong-send refunds, SIM-swap account takeovers and money-mule networks cause losses that are almost impossible to reverse.

5
00:00:28,530 --> 00:00:33,621
Risk teams review alerts by hand, slowly, and with little context.

6
00:00:33,621 --> 00:00:42,605
Shurokkha answers three questions for every transfer, in milliseconds: what happened, why is it risky, and what should upay do next.

7
00:00:43,537 --> 00:00:45,336
This is the overview page.

8
00:00:45,336 --> 00:00:47,445
On the right is Rahima's story.

9
00:00:47,445 --> 00:00:50,766
A caller tells her she won a prize and must pay a fee.

10
00:00:50,766 --> 00:00:54,945
Shurokkha interrupts her in plain Bangla, with specific reasons.

11
00:00:54,945 --> 00:01:07,589
Below are results on a held-out test window: a PR-AUC of 0.992, 96 percent of fraud caught, and only 0.21 percent of legitimate customers flagged.

12
00:01:07,589 --> 00:01:10,284
All data in this project is synthetic.

13
00:01:11,221 --> 00:01:13,788
Every decision follows the same pipeline.

14
00:01:13,788 --> 00:01:24,830
A transfer request, point-in-time features, four plug-in detectors: rules, an Isolation Forest, a calibrated LightGBM model and mule-graph risk.

15
00:01:24,830 --> 00:01:32,434
Then a YAML policy, SHAP reason codes, the action, and analyst feedback that flows back into retraining.

16
00:01:33,359 --> 00:01:38,752
Now the customer app: a demo upay wallet, signed in as a synthetic customer.

17
00:01:38,752 --> 00:01:41,226
On the right are one-click scenarios.

18
00:01:41,226 --> 00:01:44,950
First, an everyday case: sending money to family.

19
00:01:44,950 --> 00:01:51,056
The backend scores it in a few milliseconds, finds nothing unusual, and the money is sent.

20
00:01:51,056 --> 00:01:53,231
No friction for normal customers.

21
00:01:54,151 --> 00:01:56,536
Next, an account takeover at night.

22
00:01:56,536 --> 00:02:05,208
Behind the scenes, the scenario records a SIM swap two hours ago and a PIN reset minutes ago, and the transfer comes from a new phone.

23
00:02:05,208 --> 00:02:17,994
Shurokkha holds the transfer and explains why, in Bangla: a SIM change, plus a new phone, plus a large amount matches an account-takeover pattern, and the receiver is linked to a cash-out ring.

24
00:02:17,994 --> 00:02:22,460
The customer can cancel, listen to a spoken warning, or appeal.

25
00:02:23,393 --> 00:02:30,804
The What the AI saw tab shows exactly what was sent to the scoring API, with the new phone and the new area highlighted.

26
00:02:30,804 --> 00:02:37,692
Below are every detector score, the verified reason codes, and the exact policy rule that caused the hold.

27
00:02:37,692 --> 00:02:40,726
The whole decision took only a few milliseconds.

28
00:02:41,642 --> 00:02:44,118
Not every risky transfer needs a hold.

29
00:02:44,118 --> 00:02:51,149
Here, a garments worker's wallet receives fresh money and drains it at an agent, a typical mule cash-out.

30
00:02:51,149 --> 00:02:53,389
This time the decision is a warning.

31
00:02:53,389 --> 00:03:00,993
Instead of a simple pop-up, send anyway is locked behind a cooling-off timer, which breaks the panic that scammers create.

32
00:03:00,993 --> 00:03:04,313
The customer can also hear the warning spoken in Bangla.

33
00:03:05,258 --> 00:03:13,203
Every warning and hold lands in the analyst console, ranked by calibrated risk, with customer appeals moved to the top.

34
00:03:13,203 --> 00:03:16,367
A start-here pointer opens the highest-risk case.

35
00:03:17,291 --> 00:03:25,535
The case page brings all the evidence together: the transfer, the reasons in English and Bangla, and each detector's score.

36
00:03:25,535 --> 00:03:35,731
The money-flow network shows two hops around the receiver: a dense mule community draining to agent cash-out points, which no single transaction would reveal.

37
00:03:35,731 --> 00:03:39,272
The SHAP chart shows which features drove the score.

38
00:03:40,205 --> 00:03:42,967
Now the AI investigation copilot.

39
00:03:42,967 --> 00:03:50,624
It writes a case summary grounded only in this case's evidence and the standard operating procedures, with citations.

40
00:03:50,624 --> 00:03:53,801
The language model narrates; it never decides.

41
00:03:53,801 --> 00:03:57,941
If it is unavailable, a rule-based template is used instead.

42
00:03:57,941 --> 00:04:02,694
The analyst then labels the case, and that label feeds the next retraining.

43
00:04:03,622 --> 00:04:06,293
Analysts can also ask procedure questions.

44
00:04:06,293 --> 00:04:15,121
The SOP copilot retrieves the relevant procedures with BM25 search, and answers only from them, citing each source.

45
00:04:16,046 --> 00:04:26,621
The impact page shows what this means for upay: victim loss prevented as a range with its assumptions, analyst hours saved, and a simulated return on investment.

46
00:04:26,621 --> 00:04:39,551
The lift table shows the AI adds real value: rules alone reach a PR-AUC of 0.17, while LightGBM with graph and anomaly features reaches 0.99.

47
00:04:39,551 --> 00:04:50,072
Recall is shown for every fraud scenario, and a fairness audit compares false-positive rates across division, age, KYC level, persona and tenure.

48
00:04:50,988 --> 00:04:54,766
The admin page lets the risk team adapt without redeploying.

49
00:04:54,766 --> 00:05:04,154
The decision policy is a YAML file that is validated, audited and hot-reloaded across all workers, and the model registry keeps versioned models.

50
00:05:04,154 --> 00:05:10,586
Visitors get a read-only view; changes need an admin password that is never shipped to the browser.

51
00:05:11,494 --> 00:05:23,436
Finally, a built-in user study measures, instead of assuming, how many people cancel a scam after a warning, comparing no warning, a generic warning, and Shurokkha's explained warning.

52
00:05:24,350 --> 00:05:37,816
Under the hood: FastAPI, LightGBM, scikit-learn and NetworkX on the backend, Next.js and React on the frontend, PostgreSQL and Redis for shared state, all packaged with Docker.

53
00:05:37,816 --> 00:05:45,485
In a bank-style load test it handled 800 concurrent users, and killing a worker mid-load lost zero transfers.

54
00:05:45,485 --> 00:05:46,396
Shurokkha.

55
00:05:46,396 --> 00:05:52,034
The AI explains, humans decide, and the scam is stopped before the money leaves.
